  #block(breakable: false)[
  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "D", dense: true, pad: 9.0pt)[
    Gold, silver and platinum are below hydrogen in the reactivity series, so they are not oxidised by air or water and are found uncombined.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "C", dense: true, pad: 9.0pt)[
    Bauxite is hydrated aluminium oxide, Al₂O₃·2H₂O; haematite is an iron ore, galena a lead ore and cassiterite a tin ore.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "C", dense: true, pad: 9.0pt)[
    Haematite is iron(III) oxide, Fe₂O₃; FeS₂ is pyrites, FeCO₃ is siderite and Fe₃O₄ is magnetite.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "C", dense: true, pad: 9.0pt)[
    Galena is lead(II) sulphide, PbS — a lead ore. The other three all contain iron.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "B", dense: true, pad: 9.0pt)[
    Potassium is at the top of the reactivity series among these four; copper is near the bottom.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "D", dense: true, pad: 9.0pt)[
    Sodium reacts with cold water to give sodium hydroxide and hydrogen; iron needs steam, and copper and lead do not react at all.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "C", dense: true, pad: 9.0pt)[
    Steam oxidises iron to the black magnetic oxide Fe₃O₄: 3Fe + 4H₂O → Fe₃O₄ + 4H₂.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "B", dense: true, pad: 9.0pt)[
    2Na + 2H₂O → 2NaOH + H₂: the metal displaces hydrogen from water and leaves the hydroxide in solution.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "A", dense: true, pad: 9.0pt)[
    Only metals above hydrogen in the series displace it from a dilute acid; copper is below hydrogen.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "B", dense: true, pad: 9.0pt)[
    Zinc is above copper, so it reduces the copper ion to the metal; silver, gold and mercury are all below copper.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "D", dense: true, pad: 9.0pt)[
    A displacement only happens when the added metal is higher in the series; copper lies below lead.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "C", dense: true, pad: 9.0pt)[
    The reaction is so exothermic that the iron is produced molten, which makes it useful for welding in place.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "B", dense: true, pad: 9.0pt)[
    Pure alumina melts above 2000 °C, so it is dissolved in molten cryolite and electrolysed at about 950 °C.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "B", dense: true, pad: 9.0pt)[
    A reducing agent must be above the metal it reduces; carbon lies below aluminium, so it cannot take the oxygen.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "D", dense: true, pad: 9.0pt)[
    Sodium is above carbon, so electrolysis of the molten chloride — the Down's cell — is the only practical route.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "D", dense: true, pad: 9.0pt)[
    Zinc is below carbon in the series, so coke can remove the oxygen; aluminium, magnesium and calcium are all above carbon.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "B", dense: true, pad: 9.0pt)[
    Brass is copper and zinc; bronze is copper and tin; solder is tin and lead; duralumin is an aluminium alloy.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "D", dense: true, pad: 9.0pt)[
    Bronze is copper with about 10 % tin, and it is harder and more corrosion-resistant than copper alone.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "D", dense: true, pad: 9.0pt)[
    Chromium forms a protective Cr₂O₃ skin and nickel adds toughness; together they make the steel resistant to rust.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "A", dense: true, pad: 9.0pt)[
    Layers of atoms slide easily in a pure metal; atoms of a different size distort the layers and pin them in place.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "B", dense: true, pad: 9.0pt)[
    Duralumin is aluminium with copper, magnesium and manganese; it is strong and light, which is why aircraft use it.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "B", dense: true, pad: 9.0pt)[
    Rusting needs both oxygen and water; neither alone is sufficient, which is why a dry sealed jar does not rust its contents.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "B", dense: true, pad: 9.0pt)[
    Zinc is above iron, so it corrodes in place of the iron even when the coating is scratched — sacrificial protection.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "C", dense: true, pad: 9.0pt)[
    Zinc is more reactive than iron, so it loses electrons first and the hull is protected: this is a sacrificial anode.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "B", dense: true, pad: 9.0pt)[
    Tin is below iron, so once the coating is scratched the iron corrodes first and the tin makes it worse by acting as a cathode.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "B", dense: true, pad: 9.0pt)[
    M(Fe₂O₃) = 160, so n = 0.500 mol and the ore contains 1.00 mol of iron, which has a mass of 56.0 g.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "A", dense: true, pad: 9.0pt)[
    M(Fe₃O₄) = 3(56) + 4(16) = 232, and the iron contributes 168, so the percentage is 168 ÷ 232 × 100 = 72.4 %.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "A", dense: true, pad: 9.0pt)[
    n(Fe₂O₃) = 1.00 mol, and 2Al + Fe₂O₃ → Al₂O₃ + 2Fe needs 2 mol of aluminium, which has a mass of 54 g.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "A", dense: true, pad: 9.0pt)[
    n(Na) = 0.20 mol, and 2Na gives 1H₂, so n(H₂) = 0.10 mol and V = 0.10 × 22.4 = 2.24 dm³.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "C", dense: true, pad: 9.0pt)[
    Magnesium is above carbon, so electrolysis is needed; the chloride is used because its melting point is far below that of the oxide.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "A", dense: true, pad: 9.0pt)[
    Aluminium is actually very reactive, but the impervious Al₂O₃ layer that forms at once prevents further attack.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "C", dense: true, pad: 9.0pt)[
    Aluminium oxide reacts with both acids and alkalis, which is the definition of amphoteric.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "B", dense: true, pad: 9.0pt)[
    In a displacement one metal takes the place of another in its compound; the zinc replaces the copper ion.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "C", dense: true, pad: 9.0pt)[
    Zn + H₂O → ZnO + H₂; zinc is above hydrogen but below the metals that react with cold water.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "D", dense: true, pad: 9.0pt)[
    2Cu + O₂ → 2CuO, and copper(II) oxide is black.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "C", dense: true, pad: 9.0pt)[
    Mercury is the only metal that is liquid at room temperature; lead, tin and zinc are solids.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "D", dense: true, pad: 9.0pt)[
    Zn + 2HCl → ZnCl₂ + H₂; zinc is above hydrogen, so it displaces the gas from the acid.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "D", dense: true, pad: 9.0pt)[
    Gold is at the bottom of the reactivity series among these metals.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "C", dense: true, pad: 9.0pt)[
    Roasting in air gives the oxide and sulphur dioxide, and the oxide can then be reduced with carbon or by the remaining sulphide.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "D", dense: true, pad: 9.0pt)[
    Only metals above hydrogen reduce the hydrogen ion to hydrogen gas; metals below it do not react with a dilute acid.
  ]

]

